# Digirig Lite (and other C-Media CM108/CM119 sound cards) key PTT by writing
# a HID report to the chip's GPIO. hidraw nodes are root-only by default, so
# without this rule the write fails and PTT silently does nothing -- which
# looks exactly like a wiring fault and is not.
#
#   sudo cp tools/99-digirig.rules /etc/udev/rules.d/
#   sudo cp tools/set-digirig-tx-level.sh /usr/local/bin/
#   sudo udevadm control --reload-rules && sudo udevadm trigger
#
# Then unplug and replug the cable: udev only applies rules on connect, so an
# already-attached device keeps the permissions/settings it already had.

SUBSYSTEM=="hidraw", ATTRS{idVendor}=="0d8c", MODE="0660", GROUP="audio"

# BTECH APRS-K1 PRO: reports as a generic USB audio device under its own
# vendor ID rather than C-Media's, so find_cm108() will not see it without
# this line.
#
# IT HAS NO GPIO PTT, and this line does not give it one. Settled from the
# device itself on 2026-10-02: its HID report descriptor (001f:1613, 54
# bytes) contains Input items only -- consumer-control buttons plus one
# telephony bit -- and NO Output item, so there is nothing to write a GPIO
# report to and no --ptt-gpio value can key it. Writing one returns EPIPE.
# An earlier version of this comment claimed the PRO has "a CM108-style HID
# GPIO for PTT"; that was wrong, and tools/rx_compare.py had it right all
# along ("the K1 PRO has none"). This end must be keyed by VOX, which costs
# a 0.30 s lead-in and a measured 3.2 s hang against the Digirig's ~0.13 s.
#
# The rule is KEPT anyway: it is only a permission, the node does exist, and
# fm_bench_audio.hid_output_report_bytes() is what decides whether a node
# can actually key -- so a future cable under this vendor that does have an
# output report works without a udev change.
SUBSYSTEM=="hidraw", ATTRS{idVendor}=="001f", MODE="0660", GROUP="audio"

# Re-applies the measured TX playback-level sweet spot (see
# set-digirig-tx-level.sh's own comment, and docs/open-questions.md, "FOUND
# IT: the laptop's own TX playback level...", 2026-09-20) every time a
# C-Media CM108/CM119-class sound card appears, so it survives reboots and
# cable swaps instead of depending on whatever ALSA's own alsactl
# store/restore last had it at (which is keyed on card ID/number and can
# drift if more than one identical dongle is ever plugged in). Matched on
# the exact vendor+product this was measured against (0d8c:0012, the
# Digirig Lite's own C-Media chip) rather than vendor alone -- unlike the
# hidraw rule above, this SETS a value rather than just granting
# permission, so it deliberately does not fire on an untested product ID.
KERNEL=="controlC[0-9]*", SUBSYSTEM=="sound", ATTRS{idVendor}=="0d8c", ATTRS{idProduct}=="0012", RUN+="/usr/local/bin/set-digirig-tx-level.sh %k"
